hello welcome back live to westminster  you are watching the take where we can  speak now to the shadow secretary of  state for education bridget phillipson  thank you very much for being on the  program um we've been talking about uh  the chancellor rishi sunatch a speech to  the cbi he said that the government does  stand ready to do more to help families  with the cost of living crisis what do  you want to see  well i don't quite know what he's  waiting for i mean it's all very good  and well to say that he's standing ready  but families are absolutely desperate  right now they urgently need help so  labour has said that we think the  government should bring forward a  windfall tax on oil and gas producers  who by their own admission have got more  money than they know what to do with  that will provide immediate help right  now you know i hear from pensioners  who've had to turn off the heating for  weeks now because they can't afford to  heat their homes parents going without  meals because they can't afford um to  look after their children and they're  making desperate desperate choices and  they rightly expect that in those  circumstances the government will act  immediately instead we've had you know  the government telling people various  ministers saying you know work more  hours buy value goods you know a litany  of kind of excuses i think it's  insulting and out of touch and we need  action now um it's clear that you know  labour is really pushing the idea of a  windfall tax and by all accounts it is a  relatively popular policy as well but i  just wonder  you know this is the biggest cost of  living crisis since records began  actually um inflation up 9  40-year high  you know to put it into context you know  the windfall tax in the north sea is  going to raise two billion pounds at  best labor's green new deal would cost  28 billion pounds every year you're  gonna need 14 of them just to pay for  the green new deal i mean don't need  something else in the locker not just a  windfall tax what's the big idea well  the windfall tax would provide that  immediate support to families and  pensioners right now i think it's only a  matter of time by the way until the  government finally you know concede the  inevitable and follow labour's plan but  what's what's the big idea you want an  emergency budget you're going to need  more than a windfall tax in that aren't  you what what what else was in your  locker and labour's green investment  pledge would make sure that we properly  insulate our homes we know that there  are 19 million homes across the country  that aren't properly insulated so we are  more exposed than many other nations  because of that problem we've got around  insulated homes but for example take  child care another area where families  are facing real pressure at the moment  our youngest children at nursery but  also right around the school day or two  labour's children's recovery plan would  pump in investment to early years for  our most disadvantaged children we'd  also deliver breakfast clubs and  after-school activities that would  provide real help to families right now  and it's something from borrowing for  the you know the the child care and the  green new deal is that from borrowing so  what the government set out in their  corporate response was they allocated  money for that response for the covert  response we would have made that  decision to prioritize support for  children and families which would have  gone towards our children's recovery  plan we all know what children have gone  through during the pandemic the  sacrifices that were made and we know  the impact it's had on their learning  but alongside that right now families  are really facing the squeeze so if they  were extra help now on child care that  would make a massive difference okay now  i just want to talk to you a little bit  about uh westminster now a conservative  mp  arrested on suspicion of rape now of  course we can't comment on the  individual cases it's very important not  to  but it does feel like there's been an  awful lot of stories from sexual  harassment claims to mps resigning for  looking at porn to the most serious  allegations of all  is there a particular problem in  westminster with this kind of behaviour  well i think it it does take you to that  conclusion given what we've seen in  recent weeks i mean i am troubled that  the whip doesn't appear to have been  withdrawn in the case that you mentioned  initially i think what we also saw more  recently that's given rise to the  by-election and wakefield was a  conservative mp i was being convicted of  very serious offences but again where  action should have been taken sooner i  think you know there is a clear  responsibility that all of us in public  life are held to higher standards but i  think clearly given what we've seen in  recent weeks  there is a problem there that needs  addressing and this isn't just about the  conduct of mps of course westminster is  a workplace for thousands of people so  they have a right to feel safe what  should happen do we need to talk about  closing bars for example what should  happen  i i don't think that's the issue i think  it comes to really  a fundamental issue around power in our  society you know parliament reflects  some of the wider problems we see in  society around sexism and misogyny that  need to be tackled and yes there are  changes i think that still could be made  around the standard system in parliament  but actually what does that mean though  like what kind of change should happen  well i think there have been you know i  was involved in some of that as part of  the standards committee we have made  improvements but i think we need people  to feel confident in coming forward  where they have concerns about conduct  because as i say  westminster isn't just about mps  actually there are thousands of people  who work there and they like everyone  else have got the right to be in a safe  workplace just to show you this tweet uh  quickly i think i can do  on  i'm gonna whiz around this way instead  here we go right michael fabricant uh  the concerto mp i i'm expecting a strong  turnout of conservative mps at prime  minister's questions today not only to  demonstrate their strong support for  boris but also to prove they are not the  one told by the chief whip to stay at  home i'll be there and then a little uh  emoji as well um  is this a fair enough tweet or do you  think that it's in bad taste what's your  take on this i think it's an appalling  taste he seems to think it's funny but  i'm afraid you know serious sexual  offences aren't a laughing matter it's  just completely unacceptable i don't  know what he's thinking okay okay so  yeah you're not happy at all with that  tweet there now just finally while i've  got you i have to ask you  keith stammer of course is being  investigated uh by the police over  allegations he denies the allegations  but he has been invested by police over  allegations that he broke covered rules  he said if he's found guilty  he would resign  if that happens would you run  well there won't be a vacancy so that's  not going to come up that's not that is  not an eventuality that's going to  happen i'm confident i know it's not no  well there isn't a contest i look  forward to seeing kia stammer as the  next labour prime minister in downing  street and i think we've got a really  strong prospect to the next election  given the progress we've made in the  local elections and making that happen  that's all i'm interested in but if the  ball were to come loose at the back of  the scrum as someone once said would you  pick it up that's not going to happen  there's no vacancy i mean key has done  nothing wrong i'm confident that will be  the outcome when he is being  investigated by the police and he has  said that he would resign if he found  guilty to be fair so you know it's not  completely  a question from nowhere no it's also  being clear he's done nothing wrong i'm  confident he hasn't uh he's a man who  believes very strongly in following the  rules that are set um i look forward to  kiera as the next favorite prime  minister okay thank you very much for  being on the programme here today  bridgette

